WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.739
Raw pointers and references are not automatically unsafe.

2
00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:05.478
They become risky when their role is unclear.

3
00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:10.956
In modern C++, a raw pointer should usually mean non-owning access, not responsibility for deletion.

4
00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:15.749
A reference is often clearer when the object is required and cannot be null.

5
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:21.569
A raw pointer can be useful when missing data is meaningful or when interfacing with older APIs.

6
00:00:21.569 --> 00:00:24.650
Borrowed access must stay within the owner's lifetime.

7
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:29.443
That is easy to verify when a function uses a parameter immediately and returns.

8
00:00:29.443 --> 00:00:38.345
It becomes harder when a pointer escapes: stored in a member, returned to a caller, captured by a lambda, placed into a container, or passed to

9
00:00:38.345 --> 00:00:39.030
asynchronous work.

10
00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:45.192
Review pointer escape carefully because it often turns a local borrow into a long-lived stale access risk.

11
00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:46.904
Returned references deserve special attention.

12
00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:53.751
Returning a reference to a local object is invalid because the local object's lifetime ends when the function returns.

13
00:00:53.751 --> 00:00:58.545
Returning a reference into a container may be valid only until the container changes.

14
00:00:58.545 --> 00:01:04.022
Returning a view into temporary data can look efficient while quietly extending access beyond the source.

15
00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:05.734
Const does not solve lifetime.

16
00:01:05.734 --> 00:01:14.293
A const pointer or reference may prevent mutation through that access path, but it can still refer to an object that has already been destroyed.

17
00:01:14.293 --> 00:01:16.690
Lifetime validity and mutability are separate questions.

18
00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.429
Good APIs make borrowing short, explicit, and boring.

19
00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:24.564
If the callee may store access, say so and specify who keeps the object alive.

20
00:01:24.564 --> 00:01:27.303
If null is allowed, describe what it means.

21
00:01:27.303 --> 00:01:30.042
If ownership is transferred, use an owning type.

22
00:01:30.042 --> 00:01:35.520
Use-after-free prevention depends on separating ownership from temporary access and making that separation visible.
